      "id": "range-why-generalists-triumph-in-a-specialized-world-ch-6",
      "order": 6,
      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "The Trouble with Too Much Grit: Knowing When to Quit",
      "keyInsight": "We worship relentless grit as the ultimate moral virtue. Stick with it, never give up, push through the pain. But pushing through the pain is disastrous if you are marching directly into a swamp. Blind stubbornness keeps intelligent people trapped in dead-end careers, bad projects, and failing strategies out of pure pride. The highest achievers in history were not robotic finishers; they were ruthlessly honest quitters. They sampled widely, tested paths with small bets, and dropped unpromising pursuits the second they found a better match for their talents.",
      "content": "Think of finding your life work like hiking in an uncharted mountain range. If you pick the very first trail you see and vow to finish it no matter what, you might spend five grueling years climbing a jagged cliff that leads straight to a dead drop. Finishing the trail does not make you heroic. It just leaves you stranded at the top of the wrong peak.\n\nSmart navigators treat their early decisions as low-cost scouting missions. You walk down a path for an hour, inspect the terrain, test the footing, and check the view. If the trail turns into quicksand, you turn around without shame. Quitting a bad fit is not weakness; it is the only way to free up your legs for a path that actually leads somewhere worth going.\n\nEconomists call the alternative the sunk cost trap. Because you spent ten years practicing an instrument, managing an outdated warehouse, or studying an old trade, you feel obligated to suffer there forever. But match quality\u2014finding the sweet spot between what you enjoy and what you do well\u2014matters far more than head-down endurance. Grit is only a superpower when applied to the right vehicle.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Quitting things that are a poor fit is not failure; it is the essential first step toward finding work that fits you.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Blind persistence on the wrong path is just stubbornness in disguise. True progress requires testing different directions and walking away from bad fits.",
